WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:07.670
Welcome to CloudEvents
with Google Cloud.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:09.390
My name is Grant Timmerman.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.640
I'm an engineer on Google
Cloud, where I primarily work on

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.360
serverless and open source.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.430
In the next 25 minutes,
we'll be talking

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:21.390
about the rise of microservices
in serverless computing,

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.420
CloudEvents as application
an industry standard

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.860
for communicating events between
services, Events for Cloud Run,

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.960
how you can deliver events
directly to your application,

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.860
and finally, we'll be showing
the developer experience, along

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:38.360
with a demo.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.810
First, let's give a background
to event driven architectures

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:46.220
by talking about the
rise of microservices.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.010
Why microservices?

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.540
Well, it's easy to
describe with an example.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:55.360
Here we have Bob's Big Bad
Monolith with these effects.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.150
Your application has a global
state, increasing complexity.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:02.270
There is often
complicated orchestration

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:05.750
with non-uniform communication
between services.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:09.440
There's group scaling,
with services scaling up

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.026
based off demand of
one private servers.

00:01:12.026 --> 00:01:14.980
There's coupled dependencies,
with library dependency

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.190
applying to all services.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.440
And it's often
difficult to test,

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.120
heavily requiring box,
slow, often closed source.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:24.960
So in effect, the
application does not

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.510
follow the single
responsibility principle,

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:32.540
and can sort of be described
like a Swiss Army knife.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:35.720
That's compared to Mary's
Orchestrated Microservice where

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:38.030
we have the following upside.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:41.630
You have local state
with reduced complexity.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:43.920
A request and response
model, where you have

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.740
clear boundaries of ownership.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.370
Only pay when
receiving an event.

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:51.630
Individual scaling
and loose binding.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.920
Dynamic demand can increase
servers capacity independently

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.670
of each other.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:02.480
Local development and
testing is quick and easy.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.870
And you can easily
configure access management

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:09.400
with granular
security per service.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.630
We're in a world
where applications

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:14.470
are moving towards real
time communication.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:16.010
Well, what does this mean?

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:20.150
It means rapid delivery and
consumption of services.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:22.370
So event driven
architecture often

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:25.250
requires immediate responses.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:28.890
You have many to many messages,
where modern applications

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:31.850
expect to produce and
consume many events

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:36.830
from a variety of sources, as
well as a desire for on demand

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:40.700
scalability and cost, where
moderate applications see

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:46.680
dynamic traffic, but really
expect to see decreased costs.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:50.150
So once you have decoupled
microservices services

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:53.910
how do these microservices
communicate with each other?

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:56.870
Well, through events.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:57.910
What is an event?

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:01.100
In programming, an event
is an action or occurrence

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.840
recognized by software.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.230
It often originates
asynchronously

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:08.270
from an external environment.

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:11.270
The publisher of the
event has no expectation

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:13.310
about how the event is handled.

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:15.020
The consumer of the
event decides what

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:17.060
to do with the notification.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:18.700
And events are discrete units.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:22.960
They are isolated and
independent from each other.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:24.730
What is an event
driven architecture?

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:27.580
Well, it's based off of
indirect notification

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.262
with an intermediary or broker.

00:03:30.262 --> 00:03:34.092
It can deliver the full event
data or just a reference

00:03:34.092 --> 00:03:35.800
to the external
location, doesn't matter.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.330
It can be found.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:40.730
This is compared
to direct polling

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:44.840
between different services and
service oriented architectures

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.210
providing direct access to APIs.

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:50.790
While you can't really create
a real time system, as well.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:51.650
[INAUDIBLE]

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:52.940
Very hard.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:56.390
So events make
applications complicated.

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:58.880
But in practice,
developers simply want

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.010
to make applications
work and not

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:05.620
really think about
conceptual eventing systems.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:09.070
Managing events is
important but cumbersome.

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:12.140
As a developer, you have
questions like, what happened?

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:13.870
What type of event did I get?

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:15.250
Who sent the event?

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:17.110
Where is the event from?

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:18.279
When was the event sent?

00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:19.620
Is it a duplicate?

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.519
Is it created recently?

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:27.050
And moreover, every event
has its own different format.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:29.300
What if there was a
common machine readable

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.240
standard for events?

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.990
Well, introducing the
CloudEvents specification,

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:38.590
a common way for
describing events.

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:39.790
Here's the premise.

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:42.390
Events are everywhere,
yet event publishers

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:44.740
tend to describe
events differently,

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:46.840
especially with
respect to burgeoning,

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.410
[INAUDIBLE] formats and subtle
payload differences that

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:52.520
may break your application.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.950
So CloudEvents is
the specification

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:58.325
for describing event
data in a common way.

00:04:58.325 --> 00:05:01.400
It's brought to you by the Cloud
Native Computing Foundation

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:03.110
serverless working
group, a group

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:05.630
of developers across the
computing industry creating

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:09.140
standards for more
compatible services.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:11.370
At the core, CloudEvents
is an envelope

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:14.400
for information about
the occurrence of change

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:15.910
in a system.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:17.730
I like to think of
it like a one way

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:23.280
letter with a from
address and no to address.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.780
The CloudEvents format has
the following properties.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:29.210
CloudEvents carry
facts and metadata

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:30.640
with standard attributes.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:33.360
They do not prescribe what
you do with these facts.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:35.250
They are self-contained,
meaning you

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:37.560
don't need to attach any
information to the event.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.530
It's the whole picture.

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:42.090
The event format itself
is lightweight and unique

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:43.333
within a source.

00:05:43.333 --> 00:05:45.000
You can encode them
in different formats

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.090
without losing and modifying
information, such as JSON,

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:50.340
BSON, Avro, et cetera.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.830
And doesn't talk about
any HTTP methods,

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:59.300
API services, just talks
about the HTTP mapping.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:01.800
I think it's really easiest
to understand CloudEvents

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:04.260
by seeing some examples.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:07.182
Let's say we're a web
application on top of GCP.

00:06:07.182 --> 00:06:08.890
Here we have some
example events that you

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:10.550
might see in your application.

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:12.680
A new photo is uploaded.

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:15.180
A new generic message is
published to the topic.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:16.710
Could be a new user.

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:19.130
Or a new audit log is recorded.

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:23.910
Whatever it may be, a new VM
is spun up in the project.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:27.500
These are different
events you might see.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:29.370
So as a developer, I
have some questions

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:34.170
about the event being
delivered to my system.

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:36.570
What kind of event did I get?

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:37.970
When was it sent?

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:40.020
Who sent it to my service?

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:42.490
What's the event's
unique identifier?

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:44.490
What is the shape
of the event data?

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:46.900
And what is the event data?

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:48.960
Well, this corresponds
to the following comment

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:55.160
fields of type, time,
source ID, dataschema,

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:57.680
and the data fields.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:01.390
Now if we fade out
the question and left

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:04.830
align the properties,
you [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:06.740
and specify the
version of the payload

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:10.450
with the specversion
property, add some data,

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:11.910
we get a CloudEvent.

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:17.180
This is an example of CloudEvent
that you might see from PubSub.

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:20.740
Some more about the spec
can be seen on GitHub.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:25.000
Let's dive deeper into
that specification.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:27.250
Every CloudEvent has a set
of required and optional

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:28.240
attributes.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:31.630
There are four required
ones with some examples.

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:34.320
The ID, which is a string,
identifies the event.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:37.300
The source plus
ID must be unique.

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:40.496
The source as a URI
identifies the context.

00:07:40.496 --> 00:07:42.470
The specversion, the string.

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:46.790
The CloudEvent specversion
to be used with that event,

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:49.970
like 1.0 or 0.3.

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:53.690
Then you have the time-
the type attribute,

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:59.430
which is a string, a value
identifying the type of event.

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:03.020
The CloudEvents may have
some optional attributes

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:04.430
in this format.

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:05.705
You can have data.

00:08:05.705 --> 00:08:07.530
Well, it can be
anything, as long

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:09.080
as it's domain
specific information

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:10.205
about the occurrence.

00:08:10.205 --> 00:08:12.770
The data content
type is a string.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:19.070
The MiME Media Type RFC 2046,
such as application JSON.

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:23.210
You have your data schema,
which is a URI that identifies

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:25.580
the schema, the data attribute.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:28.780
In Google's case,
we use Proto's.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:32.890
Subject is a string, is
the subject of event.

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:35.190
It's really useful
for filtering.

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:38.400
And time is the timestamp,
the time of when

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:41.340
the occurrence happened.

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:46.090
Other attributes are allowed,
just not standardized.

00:08:46.090 --> 00:08:48.755
There are two primary
modes for including

00:08:48.755 --> 00:08:51.660
CloudEvents [INAUDIBLE]
via the HTTP protocol,

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:54.736
use binary encoding or
structured encoding.

00:08:54.736 --> 00:08:57.500
These are called content
modes in the CloudEvent

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:58.310
specification.

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:00.480
Let's go over them.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:02.810
Here on the right hand side,
we have an HTTP request

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:05.090
of the CloudEvent
using binary encoding.

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:08.210
At the top you see an
HTTP post to slash,

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:11.200
with some standard
HTTP attributes.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.620
There's some context
attributes with the HTTP header

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:18.980
prefix of ce dash, you're
free to add other headers too.

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:20.740
And then you have
the CloudEvent body,

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:22.910
which is the full HTTP body.

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:27.150
This encoding is used
by events for Cloud Run.

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:29.520
That's compared to the
structural encoding where

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:32.400
we have context attributes
under the first level

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.040
properties of the JSON object.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:37.770
And the data attribute
under the data property.

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:41.900
This encoding is used by
Cloud Functions, for example.

00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:44.840
So what does this
look like in practice?

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:48.190
Well your service will
receive an HTTP request.

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:51.040
Within that HTTP
request is a CloudEvent.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:53.980
Then, within that CloudEvent
you have some metadata,

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:57.940
use CloudEvent standard
attributes and your event

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:00.120
payload.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:01.455
Well that's the CloudEvent.

00:10:01.455 --> 00:10:04.910
It should be very clear
what a CloudEvent is.

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:08.080
Let's go and move on.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:10.890
Now let's talk about
events for Cloud Run.

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:13.910
Cloud Run is a fully
managed computing platform

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:16.660
for deploying, scaling,
to analyze applications

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:18.580
quickly and securely.

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:21.130
Use any language, abstract
infrastructure management,

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:23.515
with a simple
developer experience.

00:10:23.515 --> 00:10:27.510
It's built on the
open Knative standard.

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:30.070
Events for Cloud Run
makes it easy to connect

00:10:30.070 --> 00:10:35.440
your applications from
a variety of sources.

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:38.620
And it takes care of event
integration, delivery,

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:42.310
security authorization,
with error handling for you,

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:44.340
which all in all
improves developer

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:48.500
agility and your
application's resilience.

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:51.590
The long term vision is to
be able to deliver events

00:10:51.590 --> 00:10:58.550
from a variety of sources to
Cloud Run and custom sinks.

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:04.380
Currently you can deliver
events from Google Cloud sources

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:06.680
via Cloud Audit Logs or
Pub/Sub, to Cloud Run.

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:12.380
Going back to the event examples
we had described before,

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:15.750
whenever a new file is
uploaded to Cloud Storage,

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:19.010
a new message is being
published to the Pub/Sub.

00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:21.360
Or a new Audit Log is created.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:23.690
Now triggering Cloud
Run services directly

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:28.180
from events, via
Events for Cloud Run.

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:31.010
The way Events for Cloud
Run works is like this.

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:34.310
You can trigger your service
from a variety of sources,

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:37.910
Cloud Storage,
Pub/Sub, [? Cal, ?]

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:42.405
And your events will go
through Events for Cloud Run.

00:11:42.405 --> 00:11:46.640
And this will send an HTTP
request to your Cloud Run sink

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:49.290
with a Cloud Run payload.

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:51.100
Let's break that apart.

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:54.490
On the left you have some
event producers, our sources.

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:59.610
With sources you can
describe the event sources,

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:03.680
describe the event types, list
and describe all event types.

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:07.080
In the center you have
a Cloud Run service

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:09.630
where you can go and
manage your triggers,

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:12.450
you can create new triggers,
specifying the event source.

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:15.500
For example, Cloud
Services via Cloud Audit

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:19.680
Logs, or a custom
applications via Pub/Sub.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:21.630
And you have your
CRUD operations

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:23.690
for triggers like
listing triggers,

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:27.120
deleting triggers and
describing triggers.

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:30.210
These triggers specify why your
sink, which is your Cloud Run

00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:33.360
service, specified by name.

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:37.410
The three modes of interaction
for configuring Events

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:42.010
for Cloud Run use the Cloud
Console UI, the Cloud CLI

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:43.910
or REST API.

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:45.038
Let's go over them.

00:12:45.038 --> 00:12:47.330
With the Cloud-- the Events
for Cloud Run UI allows you

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:51.740
to easily create
triggers just on cloud--

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:53.710
console.cloud.google.com.

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:58.640
Simply specify your event
source, create and done.

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:01.430
Currently they'll be
using the Cloud CLI

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:03.830
after installing the alpha
components in GCloud.

00:13:03.830 --> 00:13:07.950
Just type gcloud alpha
events to get started.

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:09.890
Zooming into the
alpha events command,

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:15.590
we specify the trigger
name, the target service

00:13:15.590 --> 00:13:18.430
and the event type.

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:20.710
And we have some
optional parameters

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:23.670
used for filtering like
the service name, method

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:25.430
name and resource name.

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:30.750
In this case, this is a command
used for Google Cloud Storage.

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:34.450
Lastly you can use the Cloud
Run API if you really want.

00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:37.130
You might be asking, what's the
difference between Cloud Run

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:39.040
for Anthos and Fully Managed.

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:41.430
What's the difference?

00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:42.880
The differences are here.

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:46.315
In summary, Fully Managed
has a simpler trigger setup,

00:13:46.315 --> 00:13:49.970
is Google managed, but has
fewer events sources currently.

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:51.880
And you can only trigger
Cloud Run services,

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:55.590
not any Kubernetes
service or arbitrary URLs.

00:13:55.590 --> 00:14:01.308
TLDR, use events
for GKE using GKE.

00:14:01.308 --> 00:14:03.100
Next we'll be running
through the developer

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:04.840
experience, and
some useful tools

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:07.480
when using Events for Cloud Run.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:10.621
First off is the
CloudEvents SDK.

00:14:10.621 --> 00:14:13.860
The CloudEvent SDKs
are a lightweight set

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:16.200
of libraries
designed to speed up

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:18.770
your integration
with CloudEvents

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:20.330
within your application.

00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:21.990
They can do three things.

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:24.070
You can create CloudEvents,
send CloudEvents

00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:27.200
and receive CloudEvents,
a language [INAUDIBLE]..

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:28.490
There's a spec on GitHub.

00:14:28.490 --> 00:14:30.980
Practically speaking,
this means that you

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:35.760
can transform your HTTP
request from this to this,

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:37.550
extracting the
CloudEvent object.

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:40.730
These SDKs are available in the
following languages, in Node,

00:14:40.730 --> 00:14:45.690
Go, Python, Java,
Ruby, PHP and .Net.

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:48.320
The code is open
source on GitHub.

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:50.320
And here's a little
disclaimer here, [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:54.374
way I can use these
images, trust me.

00:14:54.374 --> 00:14:57.770
So within a CloudEvent,
each event type

00:14:57.770 --> 00:14:59.270
has its own data format.

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:01.520
So what's the
structure of that data?

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:03.470
Well we've described
the Source of Truth

00:15:03.470 --> 00:15:05.540
for the structure of
the data in Protobufs.

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:08.010
These Protobufs contain
the CloudEvent type,

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:10.950
which is a string, and
the CloudEvent data,

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:13.060
which is the full
message description.

00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:16.370
On right hand side, you can see
an example of a Protobuf used

00:15:16.370 --> 00:15:18.541
for Cloud Console.

00:15:18.541 --> 00:15:21.220
These have been
published to GitHub.

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:26.070
We'll be adding more event
types as new ones are supported.

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:29.640
Based off of the Source
of Truth as mentioned,

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:31.640
we have language
and code binding

00:15:31.640 --> 00:15:33.390
in seven different languages.

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:36.140
These provide types
like structs in Go,

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:38.690
TypeScript interfaces
in Node, POJOs in Java.

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:40.190
In terms of getting
started, we have

00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:43.190
a set of quick start
examples in these languages.

00:15:43.190 --> 00:15:48.030
As well as two simple
applications and a Codelab.

00:15:48.030 --> 00:15:51.300
Lets say we're a movie studio
recording a lot of audio.

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:55.090
We have hundreds of audio
files being recorded every day.

00:15:55.090 --> 00:15:57.570
How can we search
through these audio files

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:01.680
and find the texts in these
files being spoken, maybe

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:03.440
in real time?

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:07.900
So the idea is whenever I upload
an audio file to Google Cloud

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:12.210
storage, then let's use Cloud
Speech to Text to extract

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:16.200
the text from the audio file.

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:21.470
And then add the results
to Google Sheets.

00:16:21.470 --> 00:16:24.220
Let's go to [INAUDIBLE] VS
Code, where we can see the demo.

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:28.900
Now we're going to show
a quick demo and then

00:16:28.900 --> 00:16:30.000
dive into the code.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.918
On the left hand side you'll
see Google Spreadsheet.

00:16:33.918 --> 00:16:35.335
And on the right
hand side, you'll

00:16:35.335 --> 00:16:39.170
see a Google Cloud
have Storage Bucket.

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:42.710
I'm going to pass some
files and then upload them

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:44.810
into our Google
Cloud Storage Bucket.

00:16:44.810 --> 00:16:50.550
These are some audio files of
me speaking Chinese and English.

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:55.170
And so you'll see on
the left hand side,

00:16:55.170 --> 00:16:58.400
we have uploaded
these files and it's

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:00.170
been added to our spreadsheet.

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:05.720
In fact all the audio has been
recognized and transcribed

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:07.280
into text.

00:17:07.280 --> 00:17:12.380
[SPEAKING CHINESE], et cetera.

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:13.300
That's really cool.

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:15.390
How did we do that?

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:17.440
Well, let's go
dive into the code.

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:19.940
We use Events for Cloud Run.

00:17:19.940 --> 00:17:22.690
But first, let's talk
about our Cloud Run server.

00:17:22.690 --> 00:17:27.790
So we provided a
Dockerfile just specifying

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:31.420
our base image of Node: 14.

00:17:31.420 --> 00:17:34.420
And we're going to
[INAUDIBLE] start,

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:36.720
with the Screen Express app.

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:42.580
Here we have just a front
slash, a health check.

00:17:42.580 --> 00:17:46.220
And then a slash
for post request

00:17:46.220 --> 00:17:50.200
we extract the CloudEvent
from the HTTP headers

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:55.870
and then we can get the bucket
name and the object name

00:17:55.870 --> 00:17:58.950
from the CloudEvent subject.

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:02.700
And then we use the
bucket and object

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:05.760
names to get the actual speech.

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:12.050
So what this is doing is it's
calling this file, speech.js,

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:15.210
and then calling the
Speech-to-Text function.

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:17.250
So there's the bucket
and the file name.

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:24.870
It downloads the file and
then it reads the file locally

00:18:24.870 --> 00:18:28.080
and AudioBytes and Base64.

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:31.050
And then now we're going
to prepare our request

00:18:31.050 --> 00:18:34.350
to the Cloud Speech-to-Text API.

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:36.470
So we're going to
provide the AudioBytes.

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:38.900
We're going to provide
LINEAR16 encoding

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:41.510
since this is a wav file.

00:18:41.510 --> 00:18:46.690
We're primarily trying
to understand Chinese,

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:50.250
so our language code is zh-CN.

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:53.440
But we also want to
recognize English

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:58.470
and so we provide the alternate
language codes of English.

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:02.880
Then we call the API
and we join the result.

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:05.410
And then we just want
the actual string.

00:19:05.410 --> 00:19:07.690
Let's return that.

00:19:07.690 --> 00:19:11.350
Next we're going--
with that result,

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:13.580
we go and add a new row.

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:14.815
How do we do this?

00:19:14.815 --> 00:19:18.580
Well we have a different
file, sheets.js.

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:20.650
We provide a spreadsheet ID.

00:19:20.650 --> 00:19:23.320
Here we have an
add row function.

00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:26.830
Here we use a service
account credentials,

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:32.170
we put a local file we
downloaded, creds.js,

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:34.860
and we provide the
spreadsheets scope.

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:39.070
We've already shared
the Google Spreadsheet

00:19:39.070 --> 00:19:41.190
with the service account email.

00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:45.760
So it has access to that
spreadsheet that's private.

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:50.510
And then we append some values.

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:55.120
So with that spreadsheet ID, and
the range [INAUDIBLE] needed,

00:19:55.120 --> 00:19:59.260
we're going to provide
a 2D array of value.

00:19:59.260 --> 00:20:01.845
Which is just the
array of our results.

00:20:04.460 --> 00:20:08.980
And so we just
append the values,

00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:14.170
and then we say, 200 OK,
to the Cloud Run API.

00:20:14.170 --> 00:20:16.710
We're all good.

00:20:16.710 --> 00:20:18.510
And that's it.

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:21.170
Now, how did I
create my trigger?

00:20:21.170 --> 00:20:26.480
Well you can see, Cloud
Run, it's really great

00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:30.830
how triggers are separate
from your service

00:20:30.830 --> 00:20:32.600
so that you can
create and delete

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:37.410
triggers without having
to redeploy your service.

00:20:37.410 --> 00:20:49.210
So we can see here, if we go
and list all of our triggers,

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:53.060
we have a trigger here set up.

00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:56.060
And in fact we can go
create one like [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:56.060 --> 00:21:01.910
before of simply creating a
new trigger that's separate.

00:21:01.910 --> 00:21:06.710
Maybe we want to make a
separate trigger like this.

00:21:06.710 --> 00:21:11.990
And then it's really quick and
easy, in my opinion at least.

00:21:11.990 --> 00:21:20.830
It takes two or three
seconds and then it works.

00:21:20.830 --> 00:21:24.250
Sometimes it takes a little
bit longer, but done.

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:28.320
And that's how you
create a trigger.

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:30.250
And then you can list
all your triggers.

00:21:30.250 --> 00:21:32.290
And that's [INAUDIBLE] the demo.

00:21:32.290 --> 00:21:35.070
Hope you liked it.

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:39.226
I think my Chinese college
professor would be proud,

00:21:39.226 --> 00:21:43.010
and thanks.

00:21:43.010 --> 00:21:48.380
What we just saw was a demo of
deploying a Cloud Run Service,

00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:50.550
creating an event trigger.

00:21:50.550 --> 00:21:55.350
We uploaded some audio files
on Google Cloud Storage Bucket,

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:58.170
which then fired
an event, creating

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:00.300
a request to Cloud Run.

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:03.840
Which uses the Google
Speech-to-Text API

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:07.410
plus the Google Sheets API.

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:11.770
So in summary, we talked about
the rise of microservices,

00:22:11.770 --> 00:22:15.840
the CloudEvents specification,
Events for Cloud Run,

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:18.270
and a demo experience.

